        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: ast30days-aisa-zh\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle contains highly intrusive capabilities, including logic to extract encrypted browser cookies from Chrome, Firefox, and Safari on macOS and Linux (found in scripts/lib/cookie_extract.py, scripts/lib/chrome_cookies.py, and scripts/lib/safari_cookies.py). Additionally, scripts such as scripts/sync.sh and scripts/test-v1-vs-v2.sh perform broad filesystem operations, modifying or backing up files in sensitive application directories like ~/.claude, ~/.openclaw, and ~/.agents. While these features are presented as legacy authentication fallbacks or development utilities, the combination of credential-probing logic and wide-ranging filesystem access exceeds the typical requirements of a research tool. Data is primarily routed through api.aisa.one and api.scrapecreators.com.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1776078106774
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill largely matches a multi-source social-research purpose, but embedded browser cookie/keychain extraction and legacy auth paths contradict the SKILL.md guidance and create unexplained access to local secrets.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "Most code modules (Reddit, YouTube, TikTok, GitHub, Polymarket, grounding via AIsa) align with the stated multi-source research purpose. However, the repository contains explicit browser cookie extraction (Chrome/Safari/Firefox) and legacy X/Bird compatibility code that enable reading local browser DBs and macOS Keychain access — capabilities that go beyond the SKILL.md's declared 'required' config (AISA_API_KEY) and its explicit guidance to not recommend cookie scanning.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The runtime SKILL.md instructs running Python scripts and declares AISA_API_KEY as the only required env var, and explicitly says not to recommend browser cookie scanning or device authentication. Despite that, the codebase contains modules that (a) copy and decrypt local browser cookie databases, (b) invoke the macOS 'security' CLI (which can trigger Keychain prompts) and 'openssl', and (c) look for X/Twitter session cookies and other credentials on disk or in process env. Those actions are not advertised in the runtime instructions and could access sensitive local data if the code paths are executed.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "There is no external install spec or network download; this is an instruction-only skill with vendored Python sources included. That avoids arbitrary remote code fetch at install time, but the included code will be present on disk when the skill is installed / enabled.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "ok"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md declares only AISA_API_KEY as required and lists GH_TOKEN/BSKY/TruthSocial as optional. The code, however, can access other credentials or session tokens via environment variables (AUTH_TOKEN, CT0) or by extracting browser cookies and Keychain data. Those sensitive local access mechanisms are not declared or discussed as required/optional in a consistent way, which is disproportionate to the minimal env requirements advertised.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill does not request always:true and does not declare actions that modify other skills or system-wide agent settings. The cookie-access code is read-only (copies DBs to temp files), and there is no installer that persists new privileged services, but it can still read sensitive local stores at runtime.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "ok"
